  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1248 PM CDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nThere is a very high chance for the high pressure to remain dominant \nacross the southern plains for the rest of the week and through the \nweekend. This will have a very high chance of continuing to bring \nample heating across the panhandles that will cause the highs to \nrise into the 100s to 110s. The overnight lows will likewise \nincrease into the 70s across the panhandles as well. This means the \npanhandles will have a prolonged heatwave that will have major to \nextreme impacts over the remainder of this week and the weekend. \nThere is a high chance for heat advisories to excessive heat warning \nacross the panhandles each day through the rest of the week and \nweekend. The worse impacted areas will most likely be the normal hot \nareas such as Palo Duro Canyon and the Canadian River Valley. This \nheat can be lethal to anyone that does not have adequate hydration \nor cooling. So be sure to look out for each other and know the sighs \nof heat illness and heat stroke. Later this week towards Thursday \nthe high pressure center shifts to the west of the panhandles. This \nshift will allow for a limited amount of mid level moisture to move \nacross the panhandles bringing a low chance for rain showers and \nthunderstorms. These storms will most likely be high based in \nnature, meaning they will be better at producing wind than rain. \nStill such storms can bring limited and localized relief from the \noppressive heat. Such cooling will be far more miss than hit, so do \nnot plan any outdoor activities expecting relief from the rain as \nthat can lead to disaster. \n\nThe big take away is that the panhandles is most likely to see a \nvery hot prolonged heat wave with the potential for lethal heat for \nthose without adequate hydration and cooling. So plan accordingly \nand try to limit outdoor activities and work to avoid heat illness \nor heat stroke.",
